      "text": "and so on ....\n\n\n\nComplexity of Quick Sort\n======================\n\n1. Worst Case\n-------------------\n\nIn Quick Sort worst case occurs when the array is already sorted and in this case compexity\ncomes out to be O ( n^2 )\n\n\nSince the array is sorted the first element will require n comparison to realize it is already in\nthe correct position .\n\nThen the left part of the array will have just 1 element but the right part has n-1 elements .\n\nThen second element will require n-1 comparisons to realize it is in correct position and the array\nwill again have 1 element in its left and n-2 elemnts in RIGHT .\n\nLike this if we keep on splitting and comparing so the equation become :\n\nn+ ( n-1 ) + ( n-2 ) + ( n-3 ) + 2 + 1\n\nC ( n ) = O ( n^2 )\n\n\n\n2. Avg / Best Case \n-------------------------\n\nThe final complexity of quick sort is O ( n log n ) .\n\nThis can be derived by cal individual complexities of the function of quick and partition .\n\nThe quick () breaks the array in 2 parts on every recursive call . So if we assume that the\nPIVOT is always gets the MID POSITION then the function quick() will work as follows :\n\nn, n/2n n/2^2, n/3, ...... n/2^k\n\nBut tge last term n/2^k will be equal to 1 . Because if we keep on breaking the array in 2 parts\nthen array will become of 1 element .\n\nThis means that \n\nn/2^k = 1\n\nn = 2^k\n\nk = log n\n\nThis is the complexity of quick () function .\n\nThe partition() function will have a complexity of O ( n ) .\n\nThis can be derived by assuming that the PIVOT always gets MID POSITION .\n\nIN that ase both inner do while() loop will run n/2 times \n\nThis adding them we get : n/2 + n/2 ===> n .\n\nNow since we have partition () getting called from quick () on every recusive call , so we multiple\nboth their complexities to get the final complexity .\n\nC ( n ) = O ( n log n )",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "and so on ....\n\n\n\nComplexity of Quick Sort\n======================\n\n1. Worst Case\n-------------------\n\nIn Quick Sort worst case occurs when the array is already sorted and in this case compexity comes out to be O ( n^2 )\n\n\nSince the array is sorted the first element will require n comparison to realize it is already in the correct position .\n\nThen the left part of the array will have just 1 element but the right part has n-1 elements .\n\nThen second element will require n-1 comparisons to realize it is in correct position and the array will again have 1 element in its left and n-2 elemnts in RIGHT .\n\nLike this if we keep on splitting and comparing so the equation become :\n\nn+ ( n-1 ) + ( n-2 ) + ( n-3 ) + 2 + 1\n\nC ( n ) = O ( n^2 )\n\n\n\n2. Avg / Best Case \n-------------------------\n\nThe final complexity of quick sort is O ( n log n ) .\n\nThis can be derived by cal individual complexities of the function of quick and partition .\n\nThe quick () breaks the array in 2 parts on every recursive call . So if we assume that the PIVOT is always gets the MID POSITION then the function quick() will work as follows :\n\nn, n/2n n/2^2, n/3, ...... n/2^k\n\nBut tge last term n/2^k will be equal to 1 . Because if we keep on breaking the array in 2 parts then array will become of 1 element .\n\nThis means that \n\nn/2^k = 1\n\nn = 2^k\n\nk = log n\n\nThis is the complexity of quick () function .\n\nThe partition() function will have a complexity of O ( n ) .\n\nThis can be derived by assuming that the PIVOT always gets MID POSITION .\n\nIN that ase both inner do while() loop will run n/2 times \n\nThis adding them we get : n/2 + n/2 ===> n .\n\nNow since we have partition () getting called from quick () on every recusive call , so we multiple both their complexities to get the final complexity .\n\nC ( n ) = O ( n log n )",
